ost small aid to them because  the aid we find from the international  community is so little so far and if we  didn't respond well to this rotten  situation we are afraid of a famine one  reason for the lack of international aid  is that 26 years ago  Somaliland declared independence from  its crisis-ridden neighbor Somalia  Somali land is peaceful and stable but  no one recognizes its independence so it  receives little aid from outside  meanwhile more and more people displaced  by drought arrived in the villages the  Somaliland government refuses to set up  camps it fears the spreading of disease  and terrorism  so villagers bear the burden the  displaced people are all living with  other families our villages phone we're  stacked up on top of each other in the  huts and it's not like we had anything  before what will they do we ask what  should we do if it doesn't rain soon  that would be the end of our lives on  this earth  death is never far away in Borel  hospital - especially for the many  children suffering from malnutrition  most of them also have pneumonia measles  or diarrhea illnesses which can be fatal  for their weak state a Yan Li is also  fighting for survival he was abandoned  by his family left in one of the drought  villages saddled a hare took him in and  has been in hospital with him for 26  days half of his life she's named him a  Yan Li lucky man the international  community does not count those who died  because of other diseases that are not  related they don't count them as  starvation or a famine but as a matter  of fact this is a draught related death  the only pediatrician here is a Syrian  refugee it's a relief to have him here  says clinic director Ali supplies are  few and far between even though the  hospital is the largest in the whole  drought stricken region serving two  million people Somaliland is one of the  poorest regions on earth  it needs livestock to survive but this  livestock market is empty seventy-five  percent of its